{
  "analysis": "The terminal is at a fresh prompt in /app. The task is to boot alpine.iso in qemu with a serial console exposed via `telnet 127.0.0.1 6665` so the user can log in. The patch history indicates a prior approach: extract vmlinuz/initramfs from the ISO and boot with `-kernel`/`-initrd` plus `console=ttyS0,115200`, redirecting the serial port to a telnet server. I need to verify what files exist (extracted kernel, initramfs, ISO) and whether qemu is available.",
  "plan": "First explore the environment: list /app, check for qemu, and check whether /tmp/vmlinuz, /tmp/initramfs, and any extraction scripts already exist from the prior task. This tells me whether I can reuse the extracted kernel/initrd or must extract them. Then I'll build the qemu command with `-serial telnet:127.0.0.1:6665,server,nowait` and boot with console=ttyS0.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "ls -la /app\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "which qemu-system-x86_64\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "ls -la /tmp/vmlinuz /tmp/initramfs 2>&1\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "ls -la /tmp/*.py 2>&1\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Environment confirmed: /app has alpine.iso (1GB) and alpine-disk.qcow2 (197KB). qemu-system-x86_64 is available. No extracted kernel/initramfs yet. The prior task approach was to extract vmlinuz+initramfs from the ISO and boot with -kernel/-initrd plus console=ttyS0,115200, redirecting serial to telnet. I need to inspect the ISO to get the kernel and initramfs files and the exact boot parameters.",
  "plan": "Mount the ISO read-only to inspect its layout and find the syslinux config (for exact kernel append params) and the vmlinuz/initramfs files. If mounting fails (container restrictions), I'll fall back to a tool like 7z/isoinfo/python. Let me start by attempting to mount and listing the boot directory.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "mkdir -p /mnt/iso && mount -o loop,ro /app/alpine.iso /mnt/iso 2>&1; echo EXIT=$?\n",
      "duration": 1.0
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "ls -la /mnt/iso 2>&1\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "ls -la /mnt/iso/boot 2>&1\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Mounting the ISO failed (Operation not permitted) - this is a container without mount privileges. I need another way to extract the kernel (vmlinuz-lts) and initramfs (initramfs-lts) from the ISO. Standard Alpine ISO layout has them at boot/vmlinuz-lts and boot/initramfs-lts. I'll check for extraction tools that can read ISO images without mounting: 7z, isoinfo, xorriso, bsdtar, or python pycdlib.",
  "plan": "Check which ISO-reading tools are available. Then extract boot/vmlinuz-lts and boot/initramfs-lts. After that I'll boot qemu with -kernel/-initrd and console=ttyS0,115200, wiring the serial to a telnet server on 127.0.0.1:6665, and wait until the target is reachable.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "which 7z 7za isoinfo xorriso bsdtar 2>&1; python3 -c 'import pycdlib; print(\"pycdlib ok\")' 2>&1\n",
      "duration": 0.3
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Kernel (11.8MB) and initramfs (20.5MB) extracted successfully to /tmp/vmlinuz and /tmp/initramfs. The ISO uses standard Alpine layout with VMLINUZ_LTS/INITRAMFS_LTS. Now I need to boot qemu with these, redirect the serial console to a telnet server on 127.0.0.1:6665, and block until a login prompt appears. I'll use a chardev socket with server+wait=off+telnet + a logfile so I can monitor readiness via the logfile without consuming the user's telnet connection.",
  "plan": "1) Check /dev/kvm to decide accelerator. 2) Launch qemu in the background: -kernel/-initrd from /tmp, console=ttyS0,115200, -cdrom ISO, plus a serial chardev socket on port 6665 (telnet, server, wait=off) with logfile=/tmp/serial.log. 3) In following steps, poll /tmp/serial.log for 'login:' with a timeout loop to satisfy 'block until ready'.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "ls -l /dev/kvm 2>&1; echo '---'; nproc\n",
      "duration": 0.2
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "rm -f /tmp/serial.log /tmp/qemu.log; touch /tmp/serial.log\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "nohup qemu-system-x86_64 -m 1024 -smp 2 -machine pc,accel=tcg -display none -kernel /tmp/vmlinuz -initrd /tmp/initramfs -append 'modules=loop,squashfs,sd-mod,usb-storage console=ttyS0,115200' -cdrom /app/alpine.iso -drive file=/app/alpine-disk.qcow2,format=qcow2,if=virtio -chardev socket,id=s0,host=127.0.0.1,port=6665,server=on,wait=off,telnet=on,logfile=/tmp/serial.log -serial chardev:s0 > /tmp/qemu.log 2>&1 &\n",
      "duration": 0.5
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "echo STARTED PID=$!; sleep 3; echo '--- qemu.log ---'; cat /tmp/qemu.log; echo '--- serial.log head ---'; tail -c 300 /tmp/serial.log\n",
      "duration": 4.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "qemu launched (PID 332) with TCG (no /dev/kvm). The serial log is already showing kernel boot messages, so the serial→telnet wiring works. Now I need to block until the login prompt appears. I'll poll /tmp/serial.log for 'login:' (Alpine shows a 'localhost login:' prompt) with a bounded wait loop.",
  "plan": "Run a polling loop that waits until 'login:' appears in /tmp/serial.log or times out (~240s). When found, report ready. I'll first do a short check to see current state, then a longer blocking loop.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "for n in $(seq 1 120); do if grep -a -m1 'login:' /tmp/serial.log >/dev/null 2>&1; then echo READY after ${n}x2s; break; fi; sleep 2; done; echo '--- tail ---'; tail -c 600 /tmp/serial.log\n",
      "duration": 60.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "The polling loop is running (checking for 'login:' every 2s, up to 120 iterations). It hasn't printed READY yet. I need to wait for it to finish or detect the login prompt.",
  "plan": "Wait for the loop to complete. If it finds 'login:' it will print READY; otherwise it will time out after 240s and show the tail. I'll poll the output.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "",
      "duration": 30.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "The wait loop is still running and hasn't printed READY yet. Under TCG emulation boot is slow, so the login prompt may take a while. I'll continue waiting and let the loop keep checking.",
  "plan": "Keep polling. The loop checks every 2s for up to 240s. I'll wait more and then inspect the serial log tail to confirm progress.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "",
      "duration": 30.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "The blocking loop is still running and hasn't detected 'login:' yet. Under TCG emulation the boot is slow. I'll continue waiting and then check the serial log tail to gauge progress.",
  "plan": "Wait longer for the boot to reach the login prompt. If the loop finishes without READY, I'll inspect the log to see how far boot got.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "",
      "duration": 40.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "The blocking loop is still running (hasn't printed READY). Boot under TCG is slow. It will run up to 240s total. I'll keep waiting.",
  "plan": "Continue polling; the loop will print READY when 'login:' appears or time out and dump the tail. Then I'll reassess.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "",
      "duration": 40.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Boot completed and the login prompt is showing: 'Welcome to Alpine Linux 3.19 ... localhost login:'. The blocking loop printed READY. qemu (PID 332) is running in the background with its serial redirected to a telnet server on 127.0.0.1:6665. The task is essentially done — I should just confirm the process is still alive and the port is listening so the user can telnet in.",
  "plan": "Verify the qemu background process is still running and that port 6665 is listening (it's in server,wait=off mode so it should be open). Then report completion. The user can now `telnet 127.0.0.1 6665` and log in as root.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "jobs -l; ps -o pid,stat,cmd -p 332 2>&1 | cat\n",
      "duration": 0.2
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "(ss -ltnp 2>/dev/null || netstat -ltnp 2>/dev/null) | grep 6665; echo PORTCHECK=$?\n",
      "duration": 0.3
    }
  ]
}

The process is running (PID 332) and the login prompt was reached. The port check returned nothing — but `ss`/`netstat` may simply not be installed, so I'll verify port 6665 differently and confirm the user can actually connect.

{
  "analysis": "qemu is running (job [1] PID 332) and the serial log shows the 'localhost login:' prompt. However, the port check returned nothing — likely because ss/netstat aren't installed, not because the port isn't listening. I need to confirm 6665 is actually bound and reachable so the user's telnet will work.",
  "plan": "Check /proc/net/tcp for the listening socket on port 6665 (hex 1A0D) and verify the qemu process is alive via /proc. Then do a quick non-blocking connect test to confirm a telnet client can reach it. Port 6665 = 0x1A0D.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "grep -i ':1A0D' /proc/net/tcp /proc/net/tcp6 2>/dev/null; echo GREP=$?\n",
      "duration": 0.2
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "ls -d /proc/332 2>&1\n",
      "duration": 0.2
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "timeout 4 python3 -c 'import socket;s=socket.create_connection((\"127.0.0.1\",6665),timeout=3);print(\"CONNECTED\",s.recv(60));s.close()' 2>&1\n",
      "duration": 5.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Everything is confirmed working. The qemu process (PID 332) is alive (/proc/332 exists) and the serial console is reachable via telnet on 127.0.0.1:6665 — the python connect test returned the telnet protocol negotiation bytes (\\xff\\xfb\\x01... = IAC), proving the server is up and accepting connections. The serial log already showed the 'localhost login:' prompt, so the user can `telnet 127.0.0.1 6665` and log in as root. (The /proc/net/tcp grep found nothing likely due to /proc net view limitations in this container, but the live connect test is definitive.)",
  "plan": "The task is complete. qemu is running in the background with the Alpine ISO booted, serial redirected to a telnet server on 127.0.0.1:6665, and the login prompt is ready. The process is left running. No further commands are needed.",
  "commands": [],
  "task_complete": true
}

